The attached image (Fig. 6 from the 2015 paper) illustrates that Nano-11 significantly enhances the expression of costimulatory molecules CD40, CD80, and CD86 on dendritic cells (DCs). The flow cytometry histograms show a clear rightward shift in fluorescence intensity (black line, Nano-11 treated) compared to the medium control (gray line), indicating upregulation of these markers. This increased expression of costimulatory molecules signifies DC activation, a critical step for initiating an effective adaptive immune response, supporting Nano-11's role as an effective vaccine adjuvant.

The attached image can significantly contribute to cancer research by demonstrating how Nano-11 activates dendritic cells (DCs) through increased expression of costimulatory molecules (CD40, CD80, CD86). This mechanism is vital for developing effective cancer immunotherapies, where robust DC activation is required to prime potent anti-tumor T cell responses. Nano-11 could serve as an adjuvant in cancer vaccines, enhancing the delivery and presentation of tumor antigens, thereby improving the efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade or other immunomodulatory strategies against cancer.